Wes, there's a possibility that people out there listening don't know what Pandas is.

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:24.000
You would think it's pretty ubiquitous, and I certainly would say that it is,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.000
especially in the data science space.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:32.000
But I've got a bunch of listeners who listen, and they say really surprising things.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.000
They'll say stuff to me like,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Michael, I've been listening for six weeks now,

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.000
and I'm starting to understand some of the stuff you all are talking about.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.000
Why did you listen for six weeks?

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.000
You didn't know what I was talking about.

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.000
That's crazy.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
A lot of people use it as language immersion to get into the Python space.

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.000
I'm sure there's plenty of people out there who are immersing themselves

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.000
but are pretty new.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Maybe for that crew, we could introduce what Pandas is then.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.000
Absolutely.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:05.000
It's a data manipulation and analysis toolkit for Python.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:10.000
It's a Python library that you install that enables you to read data files,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:15.000
so read many different types of data files off of disk or off of remote storage

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.000
or read data out of a database or some other remote data storage system.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000
This is tabular data, so it's structured data like with columns.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.000
You can think of it like a spreadsheet or some other tabular data set.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:35.000
And then it provides you with this data frame object,

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:40.000
which is kind of pandas.dataframe, that is the main tabular data object.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:47.000
And it has a ton of methods for accessing, slicing, grabbing subsets of the data,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:52.000
applying functions on it that do filtering and subsetting and selection,

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.000
as well as more analytical operations like things that you might do

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:01.000
with a database system or SQL, so joins and lookups,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.000
as well as analytical functions like summary statistics,

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:09.000
grouping by some key and producing summary statistics.

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:14.000
So it's basically a Swiss army knife for doing data manipulation,

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.000
data cleaning, and supporting the data analysis workflow.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:23.000
But it doesn't actually include very much as far as actual statistics or models

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:28.000
or if you're doing something with LLMs or linear regression

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.000
or some type of machine learning, you have to use another library,

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:39.000
but pandas is the on-ramp for all of the data into your environment in Python.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:44.000
So when people are building some kind of application that touches data in Python,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:50.000
pandas is often the initial on-ramp for how data gets into Python,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:55.000
where you clean up the data, you regularize it, you get it ready for analysis,

00:10:55.000 --> 00:11:02.000
and then you feed the clean data into the downstream statistical library

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:05.000
or data analysis library that you're using.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.000
That whole data wrangling side of things, right?

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.000
Yeah, that's right.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:15.000
And so it's some history.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:22.000
So Python had arrays like matrices and what we call tensors now,

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:26.000
multidimensional arrays going back all the way to 1995,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:31.000
which is pretty early history for Python.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.000
The Python programming language has only been around since 1990 or 1991,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.000
if my memory serves.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:45.000
But what became NumPy in 2005, 2006 started out as numeric in 1995,

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:50.000
and it provided numerical computing, multidimensional arrays, matrices,

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.000
the kind of stuff that you might do in MATLAB,

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.000
but it was mainly focused on numerical computing

00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:00.000
and not with the type of business data sets that you find in database systems,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.000
which contain a lot of strings or dates or non-numeric data.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:10.000
And so my initial interest was I found Python to be a really productive

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.000
programming language.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.000
I really liked writing code in it, writing simple scripts,

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.000
like doing random things for my job.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.000
And you had this numerical computing library, NumPy,

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.000
which enabled you to work with large numeric arrays

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:28.000
and large data sets with a single data type.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.000
But working with this more tabular type data,

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.000
stuff that you would do in Excel or stuff that you do in a database,

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:36.000
it wasn't very easy to do that with NumPy

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:38.000
or it wasn't really designed for that.

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.000
And so that's what led to building this higher level library

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:46.000
that deals with these tabular data sets in the Pandas library,

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:49.000
which was originally focused on, you know,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:52.000
building with a really close relationship with NumPy.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:57.000
So Pandas itself was like a thin layer on top of NumPy originally.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:01.000
One thing I find interesting about Pandas is it's almost

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:06.000
its own programming environment these days in the sense that,

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:10.000
you know, traditional Python, we do a lot of loops,

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:16.000
we do a lot of attribute dereferencing, function calling,

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:20.000
and a lot of what happens in Pandas is more functional,

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.000
it's more applied to us, it's almost like set operations, right?

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:29.000
And a lot of vector operations and so on.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:35.000
Yeah, and that was behaviors inherited from NumPy.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.000
So NumPy is very array oriented, vector oriented.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.000
So you, rather than write a for loop,

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:45.000
you would write an array expression,

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:48.000
which would operate on whole batches of data

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.000
in a single function call, which is a lot faster

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:55.000
because you can drop down into C code

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.000
and get good performance that way.

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.000
And so Pandas adopted the NumPy way of,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:07.000
like the NumPy-like array expression or vector operations.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:12.000
But it's true that that's extended to the types of

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:15.000
like non-numeric data operations that you can do in Pandas,

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.000
like, you know, vectorized set lookups,

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.000
where you can say like, you would say like,

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:22.000
oh, like this, I have this array of strings

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.000
and I have this subset of strings

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:26.000
and I want to compute a Boolean array,

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.000
which says whether or not each string is contained

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:30.000
in this set of strings.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.000
And so in Pandas, that's the is in function.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.000
So you would say like, you know,

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:39.000
column A like is in some set of substrings

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.000
and that produces, that single function call

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.000
produces a whole Boolean array

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.000
that you can use for subsetting later on.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.000
Yeah, there's a ton of things

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:51.000
that are really interesting in there.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.000
One of the challenges,

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.000
maybe you could speak to this a little bit,

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:57.000
then I want to come back to your performance comment.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.000
One of the challenges I think is that

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:02.000
some of these operations are not super obvious

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:05.000
that they exist or that they're discoverable, right?

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.000
Like instead of just indexing into say a column,

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.000
you can index on an expression

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.000
that might filter out the columns

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:13.000
or project them or things like that.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:16.000
How do you recommend people like kind of

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.000
discover a little bigger breadth of what they can do?

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:23.000
I mean, there's plenty of, I mean,

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:28.000
I think there's great books written about Pandas.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:31.000
So there's my book, Python for Datasets.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:33.000
I think Matt Harrison has written an excellent book,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:35.000
Effective Pandas.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.000
The Pandas documentation I think has,

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:39.000
provides really nitty gritty detail

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.000
about how all the different things work.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:47.000
But when I was writing this book,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.000
Python for Data Analysis,

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.000
my goal was to provide a primer,

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:57.000
like a tutorial on how to solve data problems with Pandas.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.000
And so for that,

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:01.000
I had to introduce some basics of how NumPy works.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:04.000
So people, array oriented computing,

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:06.000
basics of Python,

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:09.000
so you know enough Python to be able to understand

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.000
what things that Pandas is doing.

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:15.000
But the, yeah, kind of, it builds incrementally.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.000
And so like, as you go through the book,

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:21.000
the content gets more and more advanced.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:22.000
It introduces, you learn,

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:24.000
you master initial set of techniques

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.000
and then you can start learning

00:16:27.000 --> 00:16:29.000
about more advanced techniques.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.000
So it's definitely a pedagogical,

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:34.000
you know, pedagogical resource.

