00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.000
Well, as history,

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:10.000
like backstory about Korto,

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:12.000
so, you know,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:13.000
it helps to keep in mind

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.000
that JJ created ColdFusion,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.000
which was this, you know,

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:19.000
essentially publishing system,

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:22.000
early publishing system for the internet,

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:26.000
similar to CGI and PHP

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:27.000
and other dynamic, you know,

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.000
web publishing systems.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:32.000
And so early on at RStudio,

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.000
they created RMarkdown,

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:38.000
which is basically extensions

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.000
to Markdown

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:42.000
that allow you to have code cells

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:43.000
written in R

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:45.000
and then eventually they added support

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:46.000
for some other languages

00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:48.000
where it's kind of like a Jupyter Notebook

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:49.000
in the sense you could have some Markdown

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:50.000
and some code

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.000
and some plots and output

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.000
and you would run the RMarkdown render

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.000
and it would, you know,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:57.000
generate all the output

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:00.000
and insert it into the document.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.000
And so you could use that to write blogs

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.000
and websites and everything.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:06.000
But RMarkdown was written in R

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.000
and so that limited,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.000
in a sense, like,

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.000
it made it harder to install

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:12.000
because you would have to install R

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.000
to use it.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.000
And also people, you know,

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:17.000
it had an association with R

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:20.000
that perhaps was, like, unmerited.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:22.000
And so in the meantime,

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.000
you know, with everything that's happened

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:25.000
with web technology,

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.000
there's, it's now very easy

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:31.000
to put a complete JavaScript engine

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.000
in a small install footprint,

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:34.000
you know, on a machine

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.000
with no dependencies

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:39.000
and to be able to run a system

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:42.000
that is, you know, written in,

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.000
a system that's written in JavaScript.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.000
And so Cordo is completely language agnostic.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.000
It's written in TypeScript

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:51.000
and it uses Pandoc as an underlying engine

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.000
and it's very easy to install

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:56.000
and so it addresses some of the portability

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:59.000
and, you know,

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:03.000
extents that were in RMarkdown.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:04.000
But as a result, you know,

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:07.000
I think our, the Posit team

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:09.000
had a lot of, just has more than,

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:10.000
you know, a decade

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:11.000
or if you include ColdFusion,

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.000
you know, more than 25 years of experience

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:16.000
in building really developer-friendly

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:18.000
technical publishing tools.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:21.000
And so I think that it's,

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.000
it's not data science,

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.000
but it's something that is an important part

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.000
of the data science workflow,

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:28.000
which is how do you present your,

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.000
make your analysis and your work

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.000
available for consumption

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:32.000
in different forms.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:34.000
And so having this system

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:36.000
that can, you know, publish outputs

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.000
in many different places

00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:41.000
is super valuable.

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.000
So a lot of people start out in Jupyter Notebooks,

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.000
but, you know,

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.000
but there's many different,

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.000
you know, many different possible input formats.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:49.000
And so to be able to, you know,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.000
use the same source to publish to a website

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.000
or to a confluence page or to a PDF

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:56.000
is like super valuable.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.000
Yeah, it's super interesting.

